<Base title="About" description="Who I am and what I make.">
  <article class="prose">
    <header class="entry-header">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>About</h1>
    </header>

    <p>
      I'm Cole. I build small, self-contained software — a zine editor that prints a booklet you
      fold by hand, a performance instrument for a theatre piece, a study companion that reads a
      book with you one page at a time. Most of it is written for a specific problem I actually
      had, which is the only reliable way I've found to know when something is finished.
    </p>

    <h2>What the work has in common</h2>

    <p>
      <strong>It runs on your machine.</strong> Staple keeps your zines in the browser and treats
      the cloud as optional — without a server configured, the publishing features simply aren't
      there and everything else still works. Noggin is a tray app talking to one SQLite file in
      your user directory. Grown-Ass Adult has no server and no accounts. pango-bluesky has no
      database at all; it reads back its own posts to remember what it has done. I like software
      that still works when the company behind it stops existing.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>It ends somewhere physical.</strong> Staple's real output isn't a file, it's eight
      pages imposed on one sheet that you print, fold three times, cut once, and hold. Fracture's
      output is two minutes in a room with an audience. Designing toward a physical result changes
      what you're allowed to fake — the print imposition is verified against actual paper, and
      Fracture ships a preflight mode that fires all sixteen pads at once and fails the check if
      the mix would clip, because the failure mode of live performance is finding out in front of
      people.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>The features it doesn't have are also decisions.</strong> Staple's social layer is
      chronological and curatorial with no comments, DMs, notifications, or ranked feed — a shared
      rack is a corkboard for a scene, not an audience. Grown-Ass Adult never stacks up missed
      chores, because a todo list that punishes you for a bad week gets deleted after a bad week.
      Both of those took more thought than most of the things I did build.
    </p>

    <h2>What I'm working on</h2>

    <p>
      Noggin, mostly — the interesting part is the division of labor. The model teaches; the
      service owns storage, page delivery, and session state, so it can't lose your place or
      invent a page it wasn't handed. Fracture is the most recent thing to actually go in front of
      an audience.
    </p>

